oes change also  look at the bottom right here really  quick  you'll see that there was a very quick  just fraction of a second  status update that was going on if we  look inside of this terms reactive  what's actually going on here is terms  is going to update  when we hit the change button so  remember the actual update is called  update but we put a label on it called  change  we set that up in the ui however terms  is dependent on the input selection  so when we're choosing a book here the  terms are obviously dependent on that  however when we change the input  the app doesn't update and we have this  kind of both ways by putting this  inside of the isolate wrapper here  so again terms is dependent on  input dollar sign selection but by  wrapping this inside of isolate  we can have terms be dependent on input  a selection  without actually changing every time we  change this thing here  there's a little bit more a wrapping of  functionality that's going on here  like the with progress that's basically  creating that progress bar  in the bottom right so that it's really  useful with larger and more complex  apps where it can take a little bit  longer to actually  update but that way if the user hits the  change button  they know the change is actually  occurring it's just sort of a formality  here that they're setting that up inside  of  inside of isolate now the next thing  that happens inside the server  is we're creating this word cloud  underscore rep  functionality in order to make this uh  word cloud drawing repeatable  now notice this is the only thing that  we've seen inside of a server function  so far  that's not inside the reactive context  or inside the render family of functions  actually all that we've seen so far is  render plot but the reason we can do  this here is because this word cloud  underscore rep functionality  it is not dependent on any of our inputs  the terms reactive it's dependent on  the input update here as well as the  input selection  the output plot that we're going to do  below here is  dependent on the reactive which was  dependent on things that we were setting  up earlier  however word cloud underscore rep  doesn't matter  it's not dependent on anything else so  we can set that up inside the server  function  next we're going to call the render plot  function  the exact same way that we did in the  other apps  and we're going to actually use that  word cloud underscore rep  functionality that brings us our word  cloud  okay now the last thing that i want to  show you in this tutorial  is my favorite cheat sheet for shiny  there are multiple cheat sheets out  there for shiny  but this one is my personal favorite  because i find it the most user friendly  personally and obviously the link to it  will be in the description of this video  right up front it breaks down that the  structure of a shiny app is broken down  as  the server and the ui components with  some  other optional components that you can  put inside of the app folder  but at the end of the day you do have a  server component and a ui component  now if we actually start at the second  page here with the ui  there's some solid explanation here to  some of the things that you saw in some  of these apps earlier  but it breaks down that as a general  rule  you're going to start with your grand  layout as fluid page  doesn't have to be that way you have  other options but that's  by far the most common grand layout  and then like we saw in that third app  there are various ways of setting up  layouts inside of layout things like the  sidebar layout split layout  flow layout slash input panel  or just setting up various fluid rows  and then the coding framework for  setting these things up  is detailed below now if we scroll back  up to the top to look at the  description for the server component  there's  details to some of the things we've  talked about in this tutorial on the  left hand side  but what i really want to focus on is at  the upper right  the render functions so we only saw  render plot  in this tutorial but there are a variety  of other options  we have render data table render image  render plot  we've got render print and render text  for text output  then we have render table that's  different from render data table because  a data table is a more  uh interactive type of functionality  then we've also got this render ui  which is one of the most powerful things  that you can actually do inside of shiny  that's the subject for things that we're  going to see in different tutorials  similarly if we go to the bottom of the  page where reactivity  is detailed it breaks down some of the  information on the just the overall  concept of reactivity  but just remember that inside the server  component  when we're doing things that are  dependent on our inputs  we are going to have to wrap things  inside of  the render family of functions or this  reactive functionality  now we can make the reactive  functionality a little bit more  uh complex with the isolate  functionality that we saw or observe  that's something that we'll see in  future tutorials but at the end of the  day  if you took nothing away from this  tutorial just remember that we have a ui  and a server component that are separate  and then reactivity in order to create  objects in the server  which are dependent on our inputs so  thanks for watching this video  in future shiny tutorials we're going to  dive deeper into it we're going to  explore  some of the more advanced  functionalities behind it and we're  going to create these apps ourselves  step by step  but i think something that you can take  away from this is if you go to the  gallery of shiny apps  you can start if you have the basic idea  by creating very similar sorts of apps  to these ones  but then there's a whole spectrum of  more complex apps  as you move through the shiny gallery  and so  if you go through these apps and they  make sense to you  before long you're going to be able to  create very similar apps to these  so thanks for watching this video if you  enjoyed it consider  sharing it smash the like button and  then i'll see you all in the not so  distant future  until then richard on data
 

